This seems pretty wrong-headed, a definite
munchkinizing [much as was turning "destroy armor"
into a "killing it by degrees" from a binary
effect].

Destroying the chance to win the game by doing one
of the things that makes the Quest un-performable is
a _learning experience_.

Playing NetHack isn't playing "one game", it is
playing a series of games until the player has
encountered and internalized enough learning
experiences to avoid all the ways *not* to win the
game, in a single game.

*Removing* ways not to win the game, instead, in the
guise of "improvements to NetHack" is just
trivializing the game one ugly degradation at a
time, it seems to me.

A useful _improvement_ as opposed to munchkinizing,
would be solve the real problem, that of the player
being unaware the game had become unwinnable.

Better than installing ways to make sure no players
feelings are ever hurt by ignorantly making a game
unwinnable and then wandering around pointlessly for
tens of thousands of turns, would be an informative
game-appropriate-flavored message from the deity (as
a representative of "The Game") at the point the
Quest becomes un-performable, whether by
self-conversion or by the Quest portal being nerfed.

Something vaguely like: "You hear the booming voice
of <deity name> saying: 'So, worshipper, you have
chosen to abandon your vow to recover the Amulet of
Yendor, by making your Quest un-performable?  Look
no more to me then for help!'" would probably let
the player know the current game was best abandoned.

